## DESCRIPTION
## Determine a row operation that will eliminate an eliminate in a matrix.
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Linear algebra)
## DBchapter(Matrices)
## DBsection(Row operations)
## Date(September 5, 2008)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)
## KEYWORDS('matrix', 'elimination', 'row operation')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");
$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;

$A = Matrix([
    [ 1, random(-6, 6), random(-6, 6),, random(-6, 6) ],
    [ non_zero_random(-6, 6), random(-6, 6), random(-6, 6), random(-6, 6) ],
    [ non_zero_random(-4, 4), random(-6, 6), random(-6, 6), random(-6, 6) ],
]);

$k = random(2, 3);

BEGIN_PGML

Consider the augmented coefficient matrix: 

[``` [$A] ```]

What row operation will zero out (eliminate) the element in the 
[@ $k == 2 ? '2nd' : '3rd' @] row, first column?  Use the first row to perform the elimination step.

[_]{Real(-1*$A->element($k,1))} [`R`][_]{Real(1)} [`+ R`] [_]{Real($k)}
[`\to \cdot R`] [_]{Real($k)}

(For example, if the operation is [`5R_3 + R_2 \rightarrow R_2`], enter 5, 3, 2, 2 in the above boxes.)

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
The standard elimination step is [`c R_j + R_i \to R_i `] to eliminate the element with 
value [`c`] in the [`i`]th row using the [`j`]th row. 

In this case, the value of [`j=1`], the value of [`i=[$k]`] and [`c=[$A->element($k,1)]`].

Therefore the operation should be
[```[@ -1*$A->element($k,1) @] R_1 + R_{[$k]} \to R_{[$k]} ```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
